{
  "abstract": "Hadamard rotations help 4-bit weight-and-activation quantization in INT4 and MXFP4 but are weak or harmful in NVFP4. Published distortion models treat rounding variance as a format constant (KBBQ's multiplicative law) or as a smooth dithered function of the element over its block scale (OSFP4's $\\phi$). We ask what these miss about the realized deterministic error. Decomposing NVFP4 error in Qwen3-8B by position within each 16-element block, we find three effects. Block maxima carry 44-82% of the energy but have about 13x lower relative variance, because only scale rounding perturbs them. Elements below $b_{\\max}/24$ flush to zero and cause up to 43% of the noise. The 4/6 scale choice tracks the block fill. The effective per-layer variance therefore ranges from 0.003 to 0.0095, and a rotation drives every layer to about 0.0076. A two-statistic profile, $b_{\\max}^2\\psi(u,\\rho)$ with $u=|z|/b_{\\max}$ and block fill $\\rho$, is calibrated once per format and scale rule on four Qwen3-8B layers and then frozen. On unseen layers, fresh documents and full projection rows of Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B, and zero-shot on SmolLM2-1.7B (Llama family), it predicts realized NVFP4 layer noise to 0.02-0.11 dB RMS. KBBQ is off by 1.2-2.4 dB and OSFP4's $\\phi$ by 3.1-4.8 dB. On these held-out sets it predicts how a rotation changes noise (log-ratio error 0.010-0.029 vs 0.25-0.57) and the sign of that change in 97-100% of layers (KBBQ 83-93%, OSFP4-$\\phi$ 46-66%). It also matches or beats format-appropriate baselines in MXFP4 and INT4. Over all 145 Qwen3-8B layers the rotation error has median 0.0055 but RMS 0.32, from a five-layer tail: four cross-coordinate failures and one unexplained. Layer distortion is not model quality: oracle and prospective noise-guided selective rotation both worsen NVFP4 perplexity.",
